---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the GitHub Apps Directory | Must-Have GitHub Apps"
  description="Learn how Must-Have GitHub Apps evaluates permissions, pricing, privacy, setup, evidence, and long-term freshness."
>
  <section class="container page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow mono">The method</p><h1 class="display">Evidence before hype.</h1><p>
      This is an independent directory for developers discovering how much more GitHub can do than
      store source code.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="container detail-content">
    <h2>Why it exists</h2><p>
      People building quickly with AI often discover security scanners, dependency alerts, code
      review tools, and repository automation only after a stressful failure. That is a
      discoverability problem, not a character flaw.
    </p><p>
      The directory makes the map visible earlier, with enough context to choose deliberately.
    </p>
    <h2>What we record</h2><p>
      Every entry separates the product’s stated capabilities from our editorial evaluation. We
      record entry type, installation model, permissions, data access, setup and maintenance effort,
      pricing, privacy notes, limitations, alternatives, official sources, and review dates.
    </p>
    <h2>What we do not claim</h2><p>
      A listing is not an endorsement, audit, security guarantee, customer count, or promise that a
      tool fits every repository. A passing scan is evidence, not proof of safety. Unknown
      information stays unknown until it can be verified.
    </p>
    <h2>How to help</h2><p>
      Suggest an app through <a
        href="https://github.com/jordan-thirkle/must-have-github-apps/issues/new?template=app-recommendation.yml"
        >the GitHub recommendation form</a
      >, or report outdated information with the maintenance form. Useful contributions include
      official sources, permission notes, current plan limits, and honest trade-offs.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
